module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.SetTheory.Determinacy.GaleStewart
public import MathlibExt.SetTheory.Cardinal.LargeCardinal.Measurable
public import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Constructions.Polish.Basic

/-!
# Borel determinacy and analytic determinacy (wanted)

Two determinacy theorems beyond the Gale–Stewart theorem
(`MathlibExt.SetTheory.Determinacy.determined_of_isClosed`), recorded with deferred proofs:

* Martin's theorem (1975): every Borel game on any non-empty set of moves is determined. It is
  provable in ZFC but needs the axiom of replacement: Friedman (1971) showed it is not provable in
  Zermelo set theory with choice (Woodin, *The continuum hypothesis, Part I*, Notices AMS 48
  (2001)).
* Martin's theorem (1970): if a measurable cardinal exists, every analytic (`Σ¹₁`) subset of
  Baire space is determined. ZFC alone does not prove this (Moschovakis, *Descriptive Set
  Theory*, 2nd ed., AMS 2009, Exercise 6A.12).
-/

@[expose] public section

open MeasureTheory

namespace MathlibExt.SetTheory.DeterminacyWanted

open MathlibExt.SetTheory.Determinacy

universe u

/-- **Martin's Borel determinacy theorem.** For every non-empty set of moves `A`, every Borel
payoff set `S ⊆ A^ℕ` (for the product of the discrete topologies) is determined
(D. A. Martin, *Borel determinacy*, Ann. of Math. 102 (1975) 363–371; Moschovakis,
*Descriptive Set Theory*, 2nd ed., AMS 2009, Theorem 6F.1, which also allows games with rules).
The Borel sets here are those of the σ-algebra generated by the open sets, as in Moschovakis §6A;
`A` may be uncountable. -/
theorem_wanted determined_of_measurableSet_borel {A : Type*} [Nonempty A] [TopologicalSpace A]
    [DiscreteTopology A] {S : Set (ℕ → A)} (hS : MeasurableSet[borel (ℕ → A)] S) :
    Determined S

/-- **Martin's analytic determinacy theorem.** If there is a measurable cardinal, every analytic
subset of Baire space `ℕ^ℕ` is determined (D. A. Martin, *Measurable cardinals and analytic
games*, Fund. Math. 66 (1970) 287–291; Moschovakis, *Descriptive Set Theory*, 2nd ed., AMS 2009,
Theorem 6G.7, from the partition property that a measurable cardinal has by 6G.2 and 6G.5;
Woodin, *The continuum hypothesis, Part I*, Notices AMS 48 (2001)). The measurable cardinal may
live in any universe. -/
theorem_wanted determined_of_analyticSet_of_isMeasurable {κ : Cardinal.{u}}
    (hκ : MetaMathlibExt.IsMeasurable κ) {S : Set (ℕ → ℕ)} (hS : AnalyticSet S) :
    Determined S

end MathlibExt.SetTheory.DeterminacyWanted
